The green solid line with grey $95\%$ bootstrap confidence interval are shown the \cite{ACEL2005WP} 
baseline results from the VAR with four lags. In orange dashed line the IRFs VAR(8), 
and in blue solid line the VAR(12).

I start desribing Figure \ref{fig:var_irfs_lag12} from what does not change dramatically.
First, the response of money growth rate to a monetary policy shock,
consistently with the baseline VAR($4$), the effect is completed within one year.
Second, there is a liquidity effect, money growth and interest rate move in opposite direction after the shock.
Third, velocity co-moves with the interest rate which fall in response to the shock, and then rise.
Fourth, price of investment seems not to have an impact, at intermediate horizons have some divergence respect the baseline.
In general, all the point estimates across different lags lies inside the confidence intervals of the VAR($4$).

Real wage in the VAR($12$) have the hump shape respect the baseline which is flat.

Some important responses does not change in the magnitude or sign, nor in the shape, but in the timing.
Output, inflation, capacity utilization, average hours, consumption, velocity and investment 
as the lag choice increase, the timing of the formation of the hump shape, and its peack is shifted in time.
On average, the peak effect in the longer-lag VARs occurs one year after respect the baseline results, with 
the exception of consumption in the VAR($8$), response is indistinguable from the baseline.

Fed Funds rate standard deviation shock change from being a $60$ basis point at horizon $h = 0$ which then 
decrease its impact almost to $0$ in two quarters, to a more realistic $25$ basis points shock that lasts for 
five quarters and then goes to $0$.

The VAR($12$) inflation response need some more attention as it seems to have a puzzeling response 
in the first year after the easing shock happens.
It seems to be very much more intertial respect the baseline, but on the other hand is consistent with all 
the other response variables that are inertial too. Having in mind \cite{ACEL2005WP} theoretical mechanism we can interpret 
the inflation response being really slow in the first year after the shock, or even negative, because firms 
have such strong frictions that allow the portion of optimisers to re-set prices once every more than one year, 
making inflation and real variables such as output, consumption and average hours being slow to react.

Given the persistence and slow response of the variables in the VAR($12$), these can be consisntent with 
ACEL mechanism if the price stickiness is high implying low implied time duration of prices.

\subsection{Unceirtanty around longer lags estimates}
Conventional wisdom thinks that increasing lag lenght increase parametrisation, therefore reduce the degrees 
of freedom making uncertainty around the estimates larger. As well explained by \cite{Long_Lag2025}, 
this is for sure part of the story.
Misspecification lowers the variance of the residuals, and that can offset the imprecision caused by additional parameters.
As preaviously mentioned, in a bivariate system there is plenty of room to increase lag lenght without 
having big concerns relative to overparametrisation. In ACEL VAR the concerns are more than realistic, 
the parametrisation grows by a factor of three already large relative to the sample.
If coinfidence intervals were going to explode anywhere, they would do it here.
\\

They do not.
Table \ref{tab:degrees_of_freedom} sumarise the coefficients and degrees of freedom of the exercise.
The baseline exercise at $p = 4$, the VAR has $410$ coefficients, $167$ available data points and $41$ 
coefficient per regression.
At $p = 12$ the system carries 1210 coefficients, each equation is estimated on $159$ observations with 
$121$ regressors, and only 38 degrees of freedom survive.
The degrees of freedom limit, at which the number of regressors per equation $k$ is greater than the number 
of usable observations $T_u$, pointed out by \cite{Long_Lag2025} in this exercise is at $p = 16$.

Tripling the lag lenght relative to the ACEL baseline is therefore not a conservative perturbation, 
but it is nearly the largest perturbation the data permit.
This maks the exercise a demanding test for the central claim in \cite{Long_Lag2025}.

