\textcolor{red}{On average, the width of the CI decrease by XXX}
Therefore suggesting that the variance reduction mechanism 
of \cite{Long_Lag2025} operates even at the parametrisation fronteer, at least in this work.

The first objection would be that this is simply overfitting: a VAR with 121 regressors
per equation fits the sample closely, the estimated residuals shrink, and confidence bands
computed from those residuals shrink with them. The objection needs to be taken seriously,
because in-sample it cannot be dismissed; reduced misspecification and overfitting have
the same in-sample signature, namely a smaller residual variance.

What can be said here, is to point on two direction, misspecification reduction, and bootstrap.
Misspecification reduction is at the hearth of \cite{Long_Lag2025} point, 
and it is the reason why the confidence bands do not explode with the lag lenght.
The latter point is that the bootstrap bands are constructed re-estimating the entire VAR 
on each artificial sample, so the unceirtanty introduced by the parameters is propagated inot the repoted 
intervals rather than being conditioned away. If the estimator would have been unstable at $p = 12$, the
re-estimated responses would have varied wildy across draws, therefore, the bands would have widened accordingly.

In this sense, bootstrap rules out the mechanical part of the concern, since parameter unceirtanty is not being surpressed.
\textcolor{red}{What it cannot be be omitted, is that the residual variance is itself untestated, because the bootstrap resamples 
from the fitted residuals of the very model under scrunity: NON CONVITNO DI QUESTO, è DI CLAUDE}

ACEL choose the lag lenght of $p = 4$ even if the AIC pointed on usign one lag, and HQ and SC suggested to use $p = 2$.
The choice was motivated by the fact that the VAR with four lags had a better fit to the data, 
and that the IRFs were more consistent with the DSGE's theoretical mechanism.

I've checked the IC at longer lags, I find that the AIC criteria points in favour of the VAR($12$).
The results of this work suggest that the choice of $p = 4$ was not enough to capture the dynamics of the data, 
and that a longer lag length is needed to obtain more accurate estimates of the DSGE's structural parameters.


